Sean McGinnis here with a GS news update  Palmer Luckey CEO of oculus the company  behind the oculus rift has discussed his  vision for the text future according to  lucky traditional TV and monitor  displays will disappear over the next 10  to 20 years and the tech will move far  beyond playing video games lucky sees VR  becoming the direct link between all  people and media it's like a Facebook  for example completely envelop your  world during an interview at PAX East  this past weekend lucky said virtual  reality it can't just be a visual thing  if via remains a visual only thing then  certainly will never replace human touch  based interactions it sounds like  touch-based interactions are part of the  plan then the interviewer also asked  lucky about the potential for oculus  rift to make people feel isolated by  encouraging them to replace real-life  social interactions with virtual reality  video calls lucky said physically  isolated maybe but I don't think  socially isolated if anything I think VR  is one of the most potentially  connecting technologies we have out  there I guess you'll have to ask  yourself why do we care if we're  physically isolated if we're mentally  connected if you can perfectly simulate  reality why do you need to actually go  see people in real life these comments  come just after Facebook surprised two  billion dollar acquisition of oculus VR  last month and lucky seems to have put a  lot of thought into the ways VR can  affect social interactions for more on  virtual reality and Palmer Lucky's  slightly dystopian view of the future  stay tuned to gamespot com  you
 

